{
 "reading": "Asked: should Indulging Patrician go into threat-v1, and for what. It is a 3-mana Vampire (an eminence token) and a 3-to-each-opponent end-step drain gated on gaining 3 life that turn. The goldfish's branch mean life gained climbs 1.521 (T5) -> 2.603 (T6) -> 4.477 (T7) -> 7.848 (T8), so the mean gained PER TURN is ~0.8, ~1.1, ~1.9, ~3.4 on T5-T8; by Markov's bound the gate can open on at most ~26% of T5 turns, ~36% of T6 turns and ~62% of T7 turns in the goldfish, and only reaches a mean of 3 on T8, after the deck's T7-8 kill window opens. Even read UNCONDITIONALLY (classify's drain_recurring: 3, a CEILING), the best slot moves killed-by-T10 only +1.7 pts and killed-by-T6 +1.0 pt. It closes no under-target axis (threat-density is already 20 against a floor of 3); the axes under target are card-advantage, ramp, mana-base, interaction-breadth and tutors. Recommendation: NO CHANGE. If the pilot wants it anyway, the cut ranking below prices the three slots it could take.",
 "log_entries_read": [
  "007",
  "008"
 ],
 "axes_engaged": [
  {
   "axis": "threat-density",
   "verdict": "strength",
   "measured": {
    "value": 20
   },
   "reading": "Twenty drain/combat finishers against a floor of 3; Patrician would be the 21st, so it adds redundancy where the deck already has plenty."
  },
  {
   "axis": "card-advantage",
   "verdict": "weakness",
   "measured": {
    "value": 7
   },
   "reading": "Under the 10 target (the audit lists uncounted draw on Anje, Black Market Connections, Champion of Dusk and others). Patrician draws nothing, and the first-ranked cut, Twilight Prophet, is a draw engine once it ascends."
  }
 ],
 "cut_candidates": [
  {
   "card": "Twilight Prophet",
   "why": "The 4-mana flyer whose payoff waits on ascend (ten permanents). Patrician is a cheaper Vampire flyer that does the same drain-and-gain job, and the goldfish ceiling for this slot is +1.5 pts killed by T10 [+1.3, +1.8] and +0.8 pts killed by T6 [+0.6, +1.0], with extra cards by T8 -0.02 [-0.03, -0.01] and cards in hand @T8 -0.04.",
   "cost_of_cutting": "A repeatable card-in-hand engine once the city's blessing arrives, on the axis the pilot calls required ('runs out of steam post T7/8'), and an upkeep life gain that would itself help open Patrician's gate. Card-advantage is already under target at 7.",
   "orphans_stack": [],
   "difficulty": "contested"
  },
  {
   "card": "Wispdrinker Vampire",
   "why": "Patrician's nearest in-deck neighbour by function (0.97): a 4-mana flying Vampire drain. Patrician does that job a mana cheaper, and this slot gives the best ceiling of the four screens: +1.7 pts killed by T10 [+1.4, +1.9] and +1.0 pt killed by T6 [+0.8, +1.2].",
   "cost_of_cutting": "The pilot restored it on 2026-11-13, crediting it in log 008's win, though the debrief could not match the card he described to its text. It drains 1 each opponent per small creature entering with no gate, and each trigger gains 1, so it is one of the life-gain sources that would open Patrician's gate. Cutting it weakens the gate the add depends on.",
   "orphans_stack": [],
   "difficulty": "contested"
  },
  {
   "card": "Marauding Blight-Priest",
   "why": "The same slot shape: a 3-mana Vampire that turns life gain into drain. Ceiling screen: +0.8 pts killed by T10 [+0.6, +1.0], damage @T10 -0.4 [-0.5, -0.4], board power @T6 -0.21.",
   "cost_of_cutting": "It orphans checker-passed stack 014 (the Teysa burst turn). It also scales better than Patrician on this deck's many 1-life triggers (Zulaport, Celebrant, Bastion, Elas, Seeker): it drains once per gain event, against Patrician's one trigger per turn. Listed to price it; not recommended.",
   "orphans_stack": [
    "014"
   ],
   "difficulty": "painful"
  }
 ],
 "add_candidates": [
  {
   "card": "Indulging Patrician",
   "closes": "threat-density",
   "source": "prompt",
   "why": "A cheap Vampire (eminence token) with an each-opponent drain of 3 per end step on turns the deck gains 3 or more. In a real four-player pod, Tithe Drinker's extort and Malakir Bloodwitch gain per opponent, which the one-seat goldfish undercounts, so the gate opens more often than the goldfish shows. The axis it closes is already at 20 against a floor of 3, though, so it is not a clear upgrade.",
   "natural_cut": "Twilight Prophet"
  }
 ],
 "open_questions": [
  {
   "question": "What fraction of T5-T8 turns does threat-v1 gain 3 or more life at a four-player table? The goldfish keeps only cumulative means, measured against one seat.",
   "settled_by": "goldfish",
   "why_it_matters": "It decides whether Patrician is a 9-a-turn drain or a 3-mana 1/4. classify's unconditional drain_recurring: 3 makes every Patrician row a ceiling until the gate is modelled."
  }
 ],
 "gaps": [
  "No per-turn life-gained distribution exists, only cumulative means, so 'how often' is a Markov upper bound on the goldfish and not a measured rate.",
  "The goldfish counts gain against one seat, so extort and Bloodwitch per-opponent gains are undercounted for a four-player table.",
  "Bloodghast was screened and is BLIND to the goldfish, so it was not ranked as a cut.",
  "deck-audit notes that mana_analysis.json is stale for this branch's list."
 ]
}
